  "query_preview": "..Mostly sunny skies, along with the moisture still \npresent within the forecast area, will trigger isolated to low-end\nscattered showers and thunderstorms over the higher terrain this \nafternoon and into the early evening hours, as the high to our \neast moves west and strengthens. Storm motion expected to be \ntoward the northeast to east, and around 10 to 15 mph. Overall \ncoverage and motion of the showers and thunderstorms will help to \nlimit the threat for flash flooding, but if any storms start to \nback-build, this will created localized areas of higher risk for \nflash flooding.\n\nOverall forecast guidance continues to support a slow down of the\nMonsoon as the high shifts westward and strengthens over Arizona.\nThis will cause daytime temperatures to soar, with many areas \nreaching the warmest reading so far this year. Extreme Heat \nWarnings are already in effect for the Grand Canyon, below 4000 ft\ntoday, and starting 10 AM MST/11 AM MDT Friday, for Yavapai and \nnorthern Gila Counties below 4000 ft, as well for Marble and Glen \nCanyon, including the city of Page, and Oak Creek and Sycamore \nCanyons, including the city of Sedona. The Extreme Heat Warning \nwas extended today for the lower elevations of northeast Arizona, \nstarting at 10 AM MST/11 AM MDT Saturday, when daytime \ntemperatures are expected to range from 100 to 105. Currently, all\nheat warnings are set to end 8 PM MST/9 PM MST Sunday.\n\nMax daytime temperatures are expected to start decreasing next\nweek as the high pressure system is expected to shift back toward\nthe east and eventually moving into a favorable location that \nshould allow for the return of more monsoonal moisture. Some \nguidance has the happening as soon as Monday, while others delay \nthis closer to middle of the week. A slower transition could lead\nto the heat products being extended into early next week, while a\nfaster transition would favor the current Sunday evening \nexpiration. These forecast trends will be monitored over the next\ncouple of days and a we should be able to have a better idea on \nwhich trend is more likely by Saturday.",
